A||||3||ichifiero||torque converter lock up.||||1||
Z||000000||ichifiero||07-29-2001||02:07 PM||||My '85 SE torque converter has not lockedup since I bought the car. I have replaced the lockup silinoid and pressure switch, but still won't lock up. I think that the trans is getting pretty shot. I was thinking of hooking up a switch to manually lockup the torque converter. I had read something about this somewhere, but now can't find it.<BR>Thanks.<BR>P.S. I have found this site to be the coolest and most informative site around. If someonce can't solve there problems here, they're in trouble <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||07-29-2001||02:43 PM||||In third gear terminal F of the ALDL should be 12V under 35mph. Over 35 it should drop to near 0V when the TC switch in the ECM closes.<P>In your case I would suspect the brake pedal switch or the wire connector to the transmission has a problem.<P>With the car stoped and the key in run, one terminal on the harness connector for the tranmission should read 12VDC. If not then the brake switch is likely shot. (Can't be fuse... the same fuse runs all the gauges. They'd be dead too.)<P>There's some stuff in my cave about manual TC locking. I'm not a big fan of simply intalling a switch. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ichifiero||07-29-2001||05:05 PM||||Thank for the tip. I will check the wire for 12V. unfortunately, I can not check it until I replace the fuel line which was cut via hte AC pulley. I have the fuel line off so I can not turn on the key. If I do have 12V at the harness to the trans, what else would you suggest?||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierohoho||07-29-2001||09:08 PM||||I have the same problem with my 84. I had 12V at the tranny on one terminal and the other side is dead which is normal.<P>The next check I did was to hook up to terminal F at the ALDL. <P>The computer activates the TCC by grounding the "dead" side of the tranny hook-up. <P>Terminal F should turn into a good ground at about 45mph or so, I'm not exactly sure.<P>Sorry to contradict you Ogre but terminal F is not a 12v source but an ECM controlled ground.<P>I used a test light. One side hooked to a 12V(+) source and the other to term F.<P>Drive down the highway, when the vehicle reaches the proper speed the ECM will ground the TCC/term F and the test light will come on, if not use your toe to pull up on the brake pedal to check the switch adjustment, if it comes on when you do that adjust the pedal switch.<P>If no light you have trouble with either the switch, ECM or passenger compartment wiring.<P>If the light does come on, hold the vehicle at speed and lightly depress the brake pedal, the light should go out before the brakes engage, if thats what happens then I would try to see if the tranny connection is getting the good ground the same way, a test light on the "dead" side of the connection also wired to a good 12v(+) source.<P>I hope that wasn't too confusing. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'84 SE 2.5l<BR>'85 Coupe 2.5l now getting a 3800<BR>'86 SE 2.5l||||reg||1
